# Lumio Fair-Use & Event License (LFEL)
Version 1.0 — 2026

Copyright (c) 2026 Marcel / Lumio Photobooth Project. All rights reserved.

---

## 1. Preamble & Philosophy

Lumio was created out of frustration with overpriced subscription models and artificial licensing restrictions found in existing photobooth software. The goal of this project is to provide a modern, rock-solid, hardware-integrated, and freely accessible photobooth solution.

This license governs fair use: it grants unrestricted permission to use the software at events (including commercial use), while protecting the source code from being appropriated or resold by third parties as a proprietary paid product.

---

## 2. Software & Binaries Usage (Commercial Event Use ALLOWED)

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of the compiled software (executables, binaries, `.exe`, release archives) to download, run, and use it for **both private and commercial purposes**:

- ✅ **Private Events:** Use at personal parties, birthdays, weddings, family celebrations, and community events.
- ✅ **Commercial Event Use:** Operation of the software at commercial events, corporate functions, exhibitions, trade fairs, and the rental of photo booths with Lumio pre-installed.
- ✅ **Revenue Generation:** Generating income through photo booth rental, print sales, or digital downloads is fully permitted.
- ✅ **No Subscriptions or Fees:** No recurring charges, license fees, royalties, or mandatory subscriptions.

---

## 3. Source Code Terms (Non-Commercial Source Code Use)

The source code in this repository is made available under a **Source-Available** model:

- ✅ **Permitted:**
  - Viewing, learning, analyzing, and building/compiling the source code for personal and private use.
  - Applying bug fixes and modifications tailored to your personal hardware setup.
  - Submitting improvements and contributions (Pull Requests) to the official Lumio repository.
- ❌ **Prohibited (Non-Commercial Restriction):**
  - Reselling the source code or derivative works for commercial gain.
  - Incorporating the source code into proprietary, commercially distributed software products or third-party paid applications.
  - Creating and offering commercial "white-label" clones of the software without explicit prior written authorization from the author.

